 "standard_url": "https://nerdc.gov.ng/",
 "standard_text": "Use the laws of indices including negative and fractional indices; solve simple exponential equations; write numbers in standard form. (summary of the NERDC SSS 1 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free SSS 1 maths worksheets on indices: laws of indices, negative and fractional indices, exponential equations and standard form. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ",
 "key_sub": "a^(1/n) is the nth root; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Same base: add indices when multiplying and subtract when dividing.</li><li>A fractional index means a root: 8^(1/3) = 2, 8^(2/3) = 4.</li><li>Standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A &lt; 10.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>27^(2/3)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Cube root first: 3</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Then square</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 9</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>0.00045 in standard form</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Move the point 4 places</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4.5 × 10⁻⁴</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multiplying the bases</td><td>2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷, not 4⁷.</td></tr><tr><td>Making a negative index negative</td><td>2⁻² = 1/4.</td></tr><tr><td>Writing 45 × 10³ as standard form</td><td>A must be less than 10: 4.5 × 10⁴.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is any number to the power 0?",
   "a": "1 (except 0⁰)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is 16^(−1/2)?",
   "a": "1/4."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why use standard form?",
   "a": "It makes very large and very small numbers easy to write and compare."
  }
 ],
